US remote workers spend an average of 49 minutes less per day commuting — time that studies show is predominantly redirected to sleep, exercise, and family time, all of which improve cognitive performance.
Gallup's 2023 State of the Global Workplace report found employees with high wellbeing are 23% more productive and 66% less likely to seek a new job — remote work flexibility is the top wellbeing driver for knowledge workers.
The 4-day work week trials in Iceland (2015–2019, 2,500 employees) and across 61 UK companies in 2022 showed productivity maintained or improved in 92% of participating companies.
Microsoft Japan's 4-day experiment showed a 40% productivity increase — suggesting that marginal working hours in an 8-hour day are far less productive than the first 6.
